@* A self-contained "How to read the Summa" helper.
    Renders a floating circular button that stacks above the back-to-top control, plus a native <dialog>, so it is reachable on the Summa home and on every question page (covering shared links).
    It explains the classic article structure and the meaning of the project name. *@

<FloatingGuide Title="How to read the Summa" Eyebrow="A short orientation" AriaLabel="How to read the Summa">
    <Icon>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="currentColor"
             xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <path d="M15 4.7v-0.7c-1.159-1.163-2.734-1.91-4.484-1.999-0.112-0.012-0.222-0.018-0.334-0.018-0.874 0-1.657 0.394-2.179 1.013-0.556-0.617-1.357-1.007-2.249-1.007-0.090 0-0.178 0.004-0.266 0.012-1.754 0.089-3.33 0.836-4.488 1.999l-0 0.7-1 0.3v10l6.7-1.4 0.3 0.4h2l0.3-0.4 6.7 1.4v-10zM5.48 11.31c-1.275 0.037-2.467 0.358-3.526 0.902l0.046-7.792c0.885-0.835 2.066-1.365 3.369-1.42 0.806 0.054 1.534 0.303 2.159 0.701l-0.019 7.869c-0.555-0.166-1.193-0.262-1.854-0.262-0.062 0-0.124 0.001-0.185 0.003zM14 12.19c-1.013-0.522-2.205-0.843-3.468-0.88-0.056-0.001-0.108-0.002-0.161-0.002-0.66 0-1.297 0.096-1.899 0.274l0.047-7.902c0.601-0.381 1.322-0.627 2.096-0.679 1.324 0.055 2.501 0.586 3.386 1.422l-0.003 7.768z" />
        </svg>
    </Icon>
    <ChildContent>
        <p>
            The <em>Summa Theologiae</em> is built from thousands of small debates. Everything nests
            in the same order, so once you can read one article you can read them all.
        </p>

        <GuideSection Title="The shape of the whole work">
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="Parts">the five great divisions of the work.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Treatises">themed groups of questions within a part (&ldquo;Treatise on the Passions&rdquo;).</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Questions">a topic (&ldquo;The existence of God&rdquo;).</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Articles">a single yes/no dispute (&ldquo;Whether God exists?&rdquo;).</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="The shape of a single article">
            <p>Each article always moves through the same four steps:</p>
            <GuideSteps>
                <GuideStep>
                    <CueContent>Objections <GuideLatin>Videtur quod</GuideLatin></CueContent>
                    <ChildContent>
                        The strongest arguments <em>against</em> the position St. Thomas will take. He states the opposing case first, and as fairly as he can.
                    </ChildContent>
                </GuideStep>
                <GuideStep>
                    <CueContent>On the contrary <GuideLatin>Sed Contra</GuideLatin></CueContent>
                    <ChildContent>
                        A single authority (Scripture, a Council, a Father) pointing the other way &mdash; the hinge that turns the article.
                    </ChildContent>
                </GuideStep>
                <GuideStep>
                    <CueContent>I answer that <GuideLatin>Respondeo dicendum quod</GuideLatin></CueContent>
                    <ChildContent>
                        St. Thomas&rsquo;s own reasoned answer. This is the heart of the article; if you read
                        only one part, read this.
                    </ChildContent>
                </GuideStep>
                <GuideStep>
                    <CueContent>Replies to the Objections <GuideLatin>Ad Obiecta</GuideLatin></CueContent>
                    <ChildContent>
                        He returns to each opening objection and shows precisely where it went wrong.
                    </ChildContent>
                </GuideStep>
            </GuideSteps>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="Following the references">
            <p>
                St. Thomas constantly points back to earlier arguments. Those blue links are his
                cross-references, and they read like an address:
            </p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem><TermContent><span class="summa-guide__ref">Q.&nbsp;2</span></TermContent><ChildContent>Question 2 of the current part.</ChildContent></GuideItem>
                <GuideItem><TermContent><span class="summa-guide__ref">A.&nbsp;3</span></TermContent><ChildContent>Article 3 (a single dispute within a question).</ChildContent></GuideItem>
                <GuideItem><TermContent><span class="summa-guide__ref">Q.&nbsp;2, A.&nbsp;3</span></TermContent><ChildContent>Question 2, Article 3.</ChildContent></GuideItem>
                <GuideItem><TermContent><span class="summa-guide__ref">A.&nbsp;1, A.&nbsp;3</span></TermContent><ChildContent>two articles at once; each number is its own link.</ChildContent></GuideItem>
                <GuideItem><TermContent><span class="summa-guide__ref">I, Q.&nbsp;88, A.&nbsp;2</span></TermContent><ChildContent>a jump to another part (here the First Part).</ChildContent></GuideItem>
                <GuideItem><TermContent><span class="summa-guide__ref">Q.&nbsp;5, A.&nbsp;8, obj.&nbsp;3</span></TermContent><ChildContent>points at the 3rd objection inside that article.</ChildContent></GuideItem>
                <GuideItem><TermContent><span class="summa-guide__ref">Q.&nbsp;5, A.&nbsp;8, ad&nbsp;3</span></TermContent><ChildContent>points at the reply to that 3rd objection.</ChildContent></GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
            <p>
                The part labels are <strong>I</strong> (First Part), <strong>I-II</strong> (First Part of the Second Part), <strong>II-II</strong> (Second Part of the Second Part),
                <strong>III</strong> (Third Part) and <strong>Suppl.</strong> (the Supplement).
            </p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="About the Supplement">
        <p>
            St. Thomas died before finishing the Third Part, breaking off partway through the
            sacraments. The <strong>Supplement</strong> was compiled after his death by a companion,
            who assembled it from St. Thomas&rsquo;s earlier commentary on Peter Lombard&rsquo;s
            <em>Sentences</em> so the work could be completed. It picks up where the Third Part stops
            and carries the sacraments through to the last things.
        </p>
        <p>
            Its final questions are two short <strong>appendices</strong> on Purgatory, which we
            list here as ordinary questions so nothing is hidden from view.
        </p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="Who&rsquo;s who">
            <p>Following medieval custom, St. Thomas usually names his authorities by title rather than by name:</p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="The Apostle">St. Paul (the author of the Epistles).</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="The Philosopher">Aristotle, the supreme authority in reasoning.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="The Master">Peter Lombard, whose <em>Sentences</em> was the standard textbook.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="The Commentator">Averroes, the great commentator on Aristotle.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="The Theologian">St. Gregory of Nazianzus.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Dionysius">the author of the mystical works then attributed to St. Paul&rsquo;s convert (now called Pseudo-Dionysius).</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="The books he cites">
            <p>St. Thomas leans on a small library of works so constantly that he refers to them by short titles. The most common are:</p>
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem Term="Scripture">
                    quoted throughout, usually by book (Genesis, the Psalms, the Gospels, St. Paul&rsquo;s Epistles). The wording follows the Latin <em>Vulgate</em>, so a verse&rsquo;s numbering or phrasing may differ slightly from a modern Bible.
                </GuideItem>
                <GuideItem>
                    <TermContent><span class="guide-term">The <em>Sentences</em></span></TermContent>
                    <ChildContent>Peter Lombard&rsquo;s <em>Four Books of Sentences</em>, the standard theology textbook of the age. The Supplement itself is drawn from St. Thomas&rsquo;s own commentary on it.</ChildContent>
                </GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="Aristotle">
                    cited by treatise: the <em>Ethics</em> (<em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>), <em>Physics</em>, <em>Metaphysics</em>, <em>De Anima</em> (&ldquo;On the Soul&rdquo;), <em>De Caelo</em> (&ldquo;On the Heavens&rdquo;) and the <em>Posterior Analytics</em>.
                </GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="St. Augustine">
                    especially <em>The City of God</em> (<em>De Civitate Dei</em>), <em>On the Trinity</em> (<em>De Trinitate</em>) and <em>On Christian Doctrine</em>. <em>QQ. 83</em> is his <em>Eighty-Three Different Questions</em>.
                </GuideItem>
                <GuideItem Term="The Fathers &amp; the Gloss">
                    John Damascene&rsquo;s <em>On the Orthodox Faith</em>, Gregory the Great&rsquo;s <em>Morals</em> (<em>Moralia</em>), Boethius&rsquo;s <em>Consolation</em>, and the <em>Gloss</em> &mdash; the standard running commentary printed alongside the Bible.
                </GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
            <p class="summa-guide__note">
                Titles are often abbreviated in the citations (e.g. <em>Ethic.</em>, <em>De Civ. Dei</em>, <em>Sent.</em>), followed by a book and chapter number rather than a page.
            </p>
        </GuideSection>

        <GuideSection Title="A few tips">
            <GuideList>
                <GuideItem>Articles start collapsed &mdash; open the ones you want and leave several open to compare.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem>Blue references like <span class="summa-guide__ref">Q.&nbsp;2, A.&nbsp;3</span> are links; follow them, then use the breadcrumb to return.</GuideItem>
                <GuideItem>Search matches both questions and articles, and jumps straight to the article.</GuideItem>
            </GuideList>
        </GuideSection>
    </ChildContent>

    <Footer>
        <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="context.CloseAsync">Begin reading</button>
    </Footer>
</FloatingGuide>

